UTF-8 support in the ncurses interface
=======================================

Background
==========

Before this work, frob could only handle non-ASCII text (e.g. Swedish
"å", "ä", "ö") correctly when run as:

    frob -i plain -k utf-8

Running the default ncurses interface with "-k utf-8" produced garbled
output, garbled echo of typed input, and, in some cases, outright dropped
text. This was a long-standing, documented limitation (see the v0.10 NEWS
entry and the TODO comment next to get_charset_alias() in
src/osportable.cc).

The root cause, in all cases described below, is the same: frob's ncurses
backend and the shared TADS2 character-mode windowing code it's built on
(tads2/osgen3.c) were written under the assumption that one byte always
equals one on-screen column. That assumption holds for single-byte
character sets (us-ascii, the ISO-8859 family, DOS/Windows code pages,
etc.), which is all frob originally had to support, but it breaks for
UTF-8, where a single displayed character (e.g. "å", "ä", "ö") is encoded
as two bytes.

Fixing this required three separate, independent bugs to be found and
fixed, plus a build system change. Each is described below.


Build system: wide ncurses is now required
===========================================

frob now requires a wide-character-capable curses library (commonly
packaged as "ncursesw", e.g. libncursesw5-dev on Debian/Ubuntu). Without
this, curses has no API for treating a multi-byte sequence as a single
screen cell.

CMFrob.cmake now asks for CURSES_NEED_WIDE first, and falls back to a
plain curses search only if that fails to find anything (this covers
platforms such as macOS, whose system-provided "ncurses" library already
includes wide-character support under a single name, unlike Linux, which
typically packages a separate "ncursesw"). Either way, the build now
verifies at configure time that the curses library found actually
provides the wide-character API (cchar_t, setcchar(), wget_wch(), etc.),
and fails with a clear error message if it doesn't.


Bug 1: garbled game text output
================================

Symptom: any non-ASCII character displayed as one or two garbage glyphs
instead of the intended character.

Cause: FrobTadsApplicationCurses::print() (src/frobtadsappcurses.h) and
FrobTadsWindow::printStr()/printChar()/charAt() (src/tadswindow.h) worked
in terms of "chtype", curses' narrow character-plus-attributes type, and
wrote strings one byte at a time via mvwaddchstr(). A two-byte UTF-8
character was written to the screen as two independent single-byte cells,
each rendered as its own (bogus) glyph.

Fix: rewritten to use curses' wide-character API. print()/printStr() now
hand the UTF-8 string directly to a locale-aware mvwaddstr() call (which,
once linked against a wide curses library and with the process locale set
correctly, decodes multi-byte sequences itself). Per-cell operations used
for scrolling and clearing (printChar(), charAt(), the blank-fill helper)
were converted from "chtype" to "cchar_t" (curses' wide complex-character
type), built/read via setcchar()/wadd_wch()/win_wch().

Files: src/tadswindow.h, src/frobtadsappcurses.h, src/frobtadsappcurses.cc


Bug 2: garbled echo of typed input
====================================

Symptom: typing an accented character at the ">" prompt displayed garbage
or blank cells while typing, even though (per testing) the command that
was actually submitted to the game was interpreted correctly.

Cause: this is not frob-specific code but the shared TADS2 command-line
editor in tads2/osgen3.c (os_gets_process() and its helpers), which is
used any time a game reads a line of input in ncurses mode. Keystrokes
arrive one raw byte at a time from the terminal; the editor inserted each
byte into the input buffer and immediately echoed it as a separate
character, advancing the cursor one column per byte. For a two-byte
character this means the first byte gets echoed (as garbage, since it
isn't a complete character on its own) and the cursor advances one column
too many, before the second byte even arrives.

Fix: added a small state machine (S_gets_pend_buf/_len/_need) that
buffers the bytes of an in-progress multi-byte sequence across successive
os_gets_process() calls, and only inserts/echoes/advances the cursor once
a complete character has been assembled (one call to the new
gets_insert_char() helper, which moves the cursor by exactly one column
regardless of how many bytes the character took). Backspace
(oss_gets_backsp), forward-delete (oss_gets_delchar), left/right arrow,
word-left/word-right, Home/End/Kill, and history-recall were all updated
to operate on whole characters (using new utf8_fwd_len()/utf8_back_len()/
utf8_char_count() helpers) instead of raw bytes, so the cursor position
and buffer offsets stay in sync.

A new OS-layer hook, oss_utf8_mode() (declared in tads2/osgen.h,
implemented in src/osscurses.cc), tells this shared code whether the
active character set is UTF-8, so behavior for all other (single-byte)
character sets is completely unchanged.

Files: tads2/osgen3.c, tads2/osgen.h, src/osscurses.cc


Bug 3: extra spaces, mid-word corruption, and dropped text in longer
output
=======================================================================

Symptom: text longer than roughly one line would, depending on exact
length and terminal width, either show extra spaces / gaps in the middle
of a line, corrupt a word, or have its tail end silently disappear
entirely (e.g. a room description ending "... i västra väggen." would
render as "... i västra v", with the rest never appearing).

Cause: three functions in tads2/osgen3.c track the on-screen cursor
column by counting bytes instead of characters:

  - ossaddsb(), which stores newly-printed text into the scrollback
    buffer, advanced its column counter by one per byte. After printing
    a chunk of text containing N multi-byte characters, its idea of the
    current column was N columns too far to the right. The *next* chunk
    of text for that line would then be told to start drawing at that
    inflated column - which could be at or past the actual end of the
    line - causing the redraw logic to skip straight past it and never
    draw it at all. This was the cause of text silently disappearing.

  - osgen_disp_trunc(), which splits long lines into fixed-size chunks
    for display, sized its chunks and advanced the display column by
    byte count rather than character count.

  - osgen_scrdisp_txt(), which re-scans a stored line to redraw it (e.g.
    after a resize or when scrolling into view), walked the line one
    byte at a time and copied only a multi-byte character's first byte,
    silently discarding its continuation bytes.

Any one of these was enough to cause visible corruption in sufficiently
long or accented text; all three needed to be fixed together since they
share the same stored-text buffer and column bookkeeping.

Fix: all three now use the same utf8_fwd_len()/utf8_char_count() helpers
added for bug 2 to distinguish "how many bytes" from "how many columns".
osgen_scrdisp_txt(), which scans a circular scrollback buffer rather than
a flat string, uses a dedicated ring-buffer-aware variant,
utf8_fwd_len_sb(), that correctly handles a multi-byte character that
straddles the buffer's wraparound point.

Files: tads2/osgen3.c


Verification
============

All three fixes were verified against a real Swedish TADS 3 game (not
included in the repository) run interactively under ncurses via a
scripted terminal session, at multiple terminal widths, covering:

  - Room descriptions and other game text with heavy diacritic use,
    including text long enough to wrap and text long enough to exceed
    the internal chunk sizes involved in bugs 1 and 3.
  - Typing, backspacing over, forward-deleting, and arrow-key-navigating
    across multi-byte characters at the command prompt.
  - Command history recall.
  - The same scenarios with the default (non-UTF-8) character set, to
    confirm no regression for existing single-byte-charset behavior.


Known issues left to fix
=========================

  - Text grid banner windows (OS_BANNER_TYPE_TEXTGRID in tads2/osgen3.c,
    implemented by osgen_scrdisp_grid()/osgen_gridwin_write()) were not
    addressed. Unlike the scrolling text window fixed above, a text grid
    is a genuinely fixed-width array of one byte per screen cell
    (win->grid_txt / win->grid_wid), used together with a parallel
    per-cell color array. Storing a multi-byte character in it would
    need each grid cell to potentially hold more than one byte, and the
    per-cell color array to stay aligned with that - a data-structure
    change, not just a counting fix. No corruption from this was
    observed in testing (the exits/status line in the test game did not
    use this window type), but any TADS3 banner or status line that does
    use a grid-type banner with accented text longer than a few
    characters should be treated as suspect until this is addressed.

  - tads2/osgen3.c's CMD_WORDKILL handling (Ctrl-W / delete-previous-word
    during input editing, Unix builds only) classifies buffer bytes as
    "whitespace" via a plain signed-char comparison (`*p <= ' '`). Any
    byte with the high bit set - which includes every UTF-8 lead and
    continuation byte, but also predates this UTF-8 work (it affected
    single-byte extended-ASCII characters, e.g. Latin-1, just the same)
    - can be misclassified as whitespace on platforms where `char` is
    signed. Not fixed here since it's a pre-existing, narrower, separate
    bug from the UTF-8 work.

  - doc/BUGS already notes that "-i plain" is only partially supported
    and buggy; that mode was not touched by this work (it was already
    the one mode where UTF-8 worked, since it just writes bytes straight
    through to the terminal) but its other listed issues remain
    unaddressed.
